        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Aboriginal Australians</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Australian mainland, Tasmania and many islands, excluding the Torres Strait Islands; as many as 500 linguistic and territorial groups, among them the Yolngu of Arnhem Land, the Arrernte and Warlpiri of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, the Pitjantjatjara, the Noongar, the Koori and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aboriginal-tasmanians">Palawa</a>; today about a third in New South Wales and a quarter in Queensland.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">arrival 50,000–65,000 years ago and settlement of the whole continent within about 6,000 years; Tasmania cut off by rising seas; the dingo and new languages and tools about 4,000 years ago; British colonisation from 1788 and frontier conflict to about 1930; protection laws and the removal of children, from 1869 to 1970; the 1967 referendum; land rights from 1976 and native title from 1992.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">Country and the Dreaming; songlines, dance and stories; rock art and the dot painting of Papunya Tula; more than 250 languages at European contact, and sign languages; networks of trade across the continent; ancestral spirits such as the Rainbow Serpent, Baiame and Bunjil; some of the oldest continuous cultures on earth</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Aboriginal oral histories, such as those the Yolngu have passed down through hundreds of generations; rock art dated by modern techniques; archaeology and the 2021 migration models; genetic studies; the journals of the First Fleet and colonial records; historians of the frontier such as Broome and Reynolds and the University of Newcastle massacre project; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counts and language surveys.</span></div>
        </div>
